  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nExtended range model data suggests that light S-SW flow in the \nlow-levels will exist across the region from Sunday into Monday, \nas a weak area of low pressure tracks east-northeastward from the \nOzarks into northern parts of the TN Valley. This will sustain a \nwarm and increasingly moist airmass, with highs remaining in the \nm-u 90s (in lower elevations) and an isolated-widely scattered \ncoverage of mainly afternoon/early evening showers and \nthunderstorms. During the timeframe from Monday night into \nTuesday, the weakening area of low pressure to our north will \nevolve into a frontal wave as it lifts further northeastward into \nthe Upper OH Valley, with a general increase in the spatial \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms anticipated both periods as \nthe trailing cold front drifts southeastward. Whether or not the \nfront continues to advance southeastward (and brings our forecast \narea a slightly cooler/drier airmass on Tuesday night/Wednesday) \nremains up for debate, and will likely be dependent on \namplification of an upper-level trough to our north. Regardless, \nwe will advertise slightly cooler high temps (u80s-l90s) by \nWednesday.",
